### How do you read from Kafka?

You configure `format("kafka")` with the bootstrap servers and a subscription. Spark exposes each Kafka record as a row with key and value as binary, plus topic, partition, offset, and timestamp. You typically cast the value to a string and parse it from JSON or Avro.

```java
import org.apache.spark.sql.Dataset;
import org.apache.spark.sql.Row;
import org.apache.spark.sql.SparkSession;
import static org.apache.spark.sql.functions.*;

SparkSession spark = SparkSession.builder()
    .appName("NBI-KafkaConsumer")
    .getOrCreate();

Dataset<Row> raw = spark.readStream()
    .format("kafka")
    .option("kafka.bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092,broker2:9092")
    .option("subscribe", "nbi-transactions")
    .option("startingOffsets", "earliest")
    .option("maxOffsetsPerTrigger", 10000)  // back-pressure
    .load();

// Kafka columns: key, value, topic, partition, offset, timestamp
Dataset<Row> transactions = raw
    .selectExpr("CAST(value AS STRING) as json")
    .select(from_json(col("json"), txnSchema).as("txn"))
    .select("txn.*");
```

```text
Kafka record columns:
+------+----------------+----------------+---------+------+-----------+
| key  | value (binary) | topic          |partition|offset| timestamp |
+------+----------------+----------------+---------+------+-----------+
| null | {"accountId"..} | nbi-transactions|    0   | 1042 | 1720000000|
+------+----------------+----------------+---------+------+-----------+
```

### How does Spark manage Kafka offsets?

Structured Streaming tracks consumed offsets in its own checkpoint, not in Kafka's consumer group. This is what makes replay deterministic and exactly-once possible. On restart Spark resumes from its checkpointed offsets, so it neither skips nor reprocesses committed batches.

```java
// Offset management is automatic via checkpoint
transactions.writeStream()
    .outputMode("append")
    .format("console")
    .option("checkpointLocation", "s3a://nbi-checkpoints/kafka-consumer")
    .start();

// On restart:
// 1. Spark reads checkpoint: last committed offset = 5200
// 2. Resumes reading from offset 5201
// 3. Does NOT rely on Kafka consumer group offsets
```

### How do you write to Kafka?

Prepare a DataFrame with a `value` column (and optionally `key`, `topic`, `partition`, `headers`) and call `writeStream.format("kafka")` with the bootstrap servers and a target topic.

```java
import java.math.BigDecimal;

Dataset<Row> enriched = transactions
    .filter(col("amount").gt(new BigDecimal("50000.00")))
    .select(
        col("accountId").as("key"),
        to_json(struct("accountId", "amount", "eventTime")).as("value")
    );

enriched.writeStream()
    .format("kafka")
    .option("kafka.bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092")
    .option("topic", "nbi-high-value-alerts")
    .option("checkpointLocation", "s3a://nbi-checkpoints/kafka-sink")
    .start();
```

### How do you control back-pressure?

The `maxOffsetsPerTrigger` option caps the total number of records read across all partitions per trigger. This is the primary back-pressure control for a Kafka source, preventing a single batch from ingesting a massive backlog.

```java
// Without rate limit -- a 10M record backlog becomes one giant batch
Dataset<Row> raw = spark.readStream()
    .format("kafka")
    .option("subscribe", "nbi-transactions")
    .load(); // May read millions in one batch!

// With rate limit -- backlog is drained in bounded chunks
Dataset<Row> bounded = spark.readStream()
    .format("kafka")
    .option("subscribe", "nbi-transactions")
    .option("maxOffsetsPerTrigger", 50000) // max 50K records per batch
    .load();
```

### What delivery semantics does it provide?

The Kafka source with checkpointed offsets gives effectively exactly-once consumption. For end-to-end exactly-once, the sink must not create visible duplicates on retry. With the Kafka sink you enable idempotent producers. With a database sink you use an upsert by primary key.

```java
// Kafka sink with idempotent producer
enriched.writeStream()
    .format("kafka")
    .option("kafka.bootstrap.servers", "broker1:9092")
    .option("topic", "nbi-enriched-txns")
    .option("kafka.enable.idempotence", "true")  // idempotent producer
    .option("checkpointLocation", "s3a://nbi-checkpoints/enriched")
    .start();

// Database sink with foreachBatch upsert
counts.writeStream()
    .foreachBatch((batchDf, batchId) -> {
        batchDf.write()
            .format("jdbc")
            .option("url", "jdbc:postgresql://nbi-db:5432/analytics")
            .option("dbtable", "account_counts")
            .mode("append")  // with ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE
            .save();
    })
    .option("checkpointLocation", "s3a://nbi-checkpoints/db-upsert")
    .start();
```

### How is this different from Kafka Streams?

| Aspect | Structured Streaming + Kafka | Kafka Streams |
|---|---|---|
| Engine | Spark (distributed batch engine) | Kafka client library |
| Deployment | Cluster (YARN/K8s/standalone) | Embedded in JVM app |
| Latency | Micro-batch (100ms-seconds) | Record-by-record (ms) |
| State | Spark state store / RocksDB | RocksDB per partition |
| SQL support | Full Spark SQL | KSQL (separate) |
| Best for | Complex analytics, ML enrichment | Simple event-driven transforms |

```java
// Structured Streaming: cluster-scale, SQL-native
transactions.groupBy("accountId").count();

// Kafka Streams: lightweight, embedded
// KStream<String, Transaction> stream = builder.stream("nbi-transactions");
// stream.groupByKey().count();
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is claiming exactly-once without making the sink idempotent. Spark guarantees it will replay uncommitted batches, but if the sink creates duplicates on replay, the end-to-end result is at-least-once.

```java
// WRONG -- non-idempotent sink, duplicates on replay
counts.writeStream()
    .foreach(new ForeachWriter<Row>() {
        public boolean open(long partitionId, long epochId) { return true; }
        public void process(Row row) {
            // INSERT into database without ON CONFLICT
            // Replay creates duplicate rows!
        }
        public void close(Throwable error) {}
    })
    .start();

// CORRECT -- idempotent upsert
counts.writeStream()
    .foreachBatch((df, batchId) -> {
        // UPSERT with ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE
        df.write().format("jdbc")
            .option("dbtable", "account_counts")
            .mode("append").save();
    }).start();
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

End-to-end exactly-once is a property of the whole chain, not just Spark. Spark guarantees it will reprocess uncommitted batches on recovery. The sink must guarantee that reprocessing does not produce duplicate effects. For a Kafka-to-Kafka pipeline that means idempotent producers plus idempotent consumers keyed on a stable business id such as a transaction id. Designing the sink for idempotency is the real work of exactly-once.
